Cubic volume mapping as PulseAudio and PipeWire define it.

Upstream's CubicMapping is a port of alsamixer's variant: it offsets the
slider fraction by min_norm = 10^(-db_range/60) before cubing, so the curve
bottoms out at exactly -db_range instead of running off to -inf. Every
desktop mixer the user actually has does the plain cube - amplitude is the
fraction cubed, so dB = 60*log10(fraction). The offset makes the same number
on the dial about 7 dB louder here than on the same number in PipeWire, and
on a speaker whose whole point is that its dial should feel like the dial
next to it, that is the wrong curve.

Checked against a live pipewire 1.6.8 sink rather than from memory: it
reported 9830 / 15% / -49.44 dB, and 60*log10(0.15) is -49.44.

The second hunk is the consequence. On a control with a finite range the
plain cube legitimately asks for less than the floor - 5% of a 60 dB
control wants -78 dB - and the raw index comes out negative. set_volume
fed that straight into an .expect(), which panics and takes librespot with
it. Clamped to the control's own range instead; alsa's softvol turns index
0 into real digital silence, which is what a hardware mixer with the same
range would do anyway.

---
diff --git a/playback/src/mixer/alsamixer.rs b/playback/src/mixer/alsamixer.rs
index 90daaf1..2c8b743 100644
--- a/playback/src/mixer/alsamixer.rs
+++ b/playback/src/mixer/alsamixer.rs
@@ -267,7 +267,11 @@ impl Mixer for AlsaMixer {
         }
 
         if self.is_softvol {
-            let scaled_volume = (self.min as f64 + mapped_volume * self.range as f64) as i64;
+            // Clamp: the cubic curve legitimately maps the bottom of the slider
+            // below this control's floor, and a negative index would come back
+            // as an error that the .expect() below turns into a panic.
+            let scaled_volume = (self.min as f64 + mapped_volume * self.range as f64)
+                .clamp(self.min as f64, self.max as f64) as i64;
             debug!("Setting Alsa raw volume to {}", scaled_volume);
             simple_element
                 .set_playback_volume_all(scaled_volume)
diff --git a/playback/src/mixer/mappings.rs b/playback/src/mixer/mappings.rs
index cb238a2..62b489e 100644
--- a/playback/src/mixer/mappings.rs
+++ b/playback/src/mixer/mappings.rs
@@ -123,35 +123,29 @@ impl LogMapping {
     }
 }
 
-// Ported from: https://github.com/alsa-project/alsa-utils/blob/master/alsamixer/volume_mapping.c
-// which in turn was inspired by: https://www.robotplanet.dk/audio/audio_gui_design/
+// The cube that PulseAudio and PipeWire put behind every desktop volume slider:
+// amplitude is the slider fraction cubed, so dB = 60 * log10(fraction). Measured
+// against a real pipewire 1.6.8 sink to be sure - it reported 9830 / 15% /
+// -49.44 dB, and 60 * log10(0.15) is -49.44.
 //
-// Though this mapping is computationally less expensive than the logarithmic
-// mapping, it really does not matter as librespot memoizes the mapped value.
-// Use this mapping if you have some reason to mimic Alsa's native mixer or
-// prefer a more granular control in the upper volume range.
+// Upstream ported alsamixer's variant instead, which offsets the fraction by
+// min_norm = 10^(-db_range/60) so the curve bottoms out at exactly -db_range
+// rather than -inf. That offset is why 25% landed on -29.3 dB here where every
+// desktop puts -36.1, and the whole point on this device is that the speaker's
+// dial should feel like the dial the user already has. Dropped, along with
+// min_norm.
 //
-// Note: https://www.dr-lex.be/info-stuff/volumecontrols.html#ideal3 shows
-// better approximations to the logarithmic curve but because we only intend
-// to mimic Alsa here, we do not implement them. If your desire is to use a
-// logarithmic mapping, then use that volume control.
+// Consequence to keep in mind: on a control with a finite range this really does
+// run off the bottom. Below 10% of a 60 dB control the mapped value is under the
+// floor, so it mutes - which is what the maths says and what a hardware mixer
+// with the same range would do.
 pub struct CubicMapping {}
 impl VolumeMapping for CubicMapping {
-    fn linear_to_mapped(normalized_volume: f64, db_range: f64) -> f64 {
-        let min_norm = Self::min_norm(db_range);
-        f64::powi(normalized_volume * (1.0 - min_norm) + min_norm, 3)
+    fn linear_to_mapped(normalized_volume: f64, _db_range: f64) -> f64 {
+        f64::powi(normalized_volume, 3)
     }
 
-    fn mapped_to_linear(mapped_volume: f64, db_range: f64) -> f64 {
-        let min_norm = Self::min_norm(db_range);
-        (mapped_volume.powf(1.0 / 3.0) - min_norm) / (1.0 - min_norm)
-    }
-}
-
-impl CubicMapping {
-    fn min_norm(db_range: f64) -> f64 {
-        // Note that this 60.0 is unrelated to DEFAULT_DB_RANGE.
-        // Instead, it's the cubic voltage to dB ratio.
-        f64::powf(10.0, -db_range / 60.0)
+    fn mapped_to_linear(mapped_volume: f64, _db_range: f64) -> f64 {
+        mapped_volume.powf(1.0 / 3.0)
     }
 }
